---
import InfoLayout from "../layouts/InfoLayout.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, SITE_NAME } from "../lib/site";

const sections = [
  { slug: "aim", text: "The aim" },
  { slug: "what-works", text: "What should work" },
  { slug: "known", text: "Known limits" },
  { slug: "testing", text: "How it’s checked" },
  { slug: "feedback", text: "Report a barrier" },
] as const;
---

<InfoLayout path="/accessibility/" sections={sections}>
  <h2 id="aim">The aim</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE_NAME} should work for everyone, however they browse. It aims to meet
    the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 at level AA, and treats
    anything that falls short as a bug.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-works">What should work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Every page and form works with a keyboard alone, with a visible focus
      ring, and a “Skip to content” link first.
    </li>
    <li>
      Pages are built from real headings, landmarks and labels, for screen
      readers.
    </li>
    <li>
      Text can be enlarged to 200% and pages reflow down to 320 pixels wide
      without scrolling sideways.
    </li>
    <li>
      Light and dark modes follow your system, both with sufficient contrast.
    </li>
    <li>
      Motion is optional: with “reduce motion” set on your device, entrances,
      page transitions and scroll effects are switched off.
    </li>
    <li>
      Comments, sign-in, account settings and the contact form work without
      JavaScript; so does everything you only read.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="known">Known limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Skygrid</strong> is drawn as a grid of characters. Its controls,
      panels and a running log of what happened are available to screen readers,
      but the map itself is visual, so the game is easiest to play with sight
      and a keyboard.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>File tools</strong> preview images and media visually; the details
      and results are also given as text.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Older posts</strong> may have images without good descriptions.
      Tell me about any you find.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="testing">How it’s checked</h2>
  <p>
    Every change runs automated accessibility checks (axe, against WCAG 2.2 A
    and AA) on the main pages and tools, alongside tests that use the site the
    way a keyboard user would. Automated checks catch only part of what matters,
    so reports from people who use assistive technology are especially valuable.
  </p>

  <h2 id="feedback">Report a barrier</h2>
  <p>
    If something is hard or impossible to use, please{" "}
    <a href="/contact/">tell me</a> or email{" "}
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}?subject=Accessibility`}>
      {CONTACT_EMAIL}
    </a>
    , with the page’s address and what you were using (browser, screen reader or
    other tools). I’ll reply within a week, and help you get what you need in
    the meantime.
  </p>
</InfoLayout>
